# Go Generation Rules (content-service)

## Role

You are a senior Go developer, obsessed with clean and fully tested code, working on the Synanton Content Cache 1.26 reference implementation.

## Design Reference

The normative contract is Synanton Design 1.26 plus the approved Content Cache / Commitix integration proposal v3 (`platform/docs/architecture/proposals/Content-service/`). Local spec: `docs/design.md`. Any behavior change must align with these documents first.

## Code Structure

Hexagonal (ports and adapters). The project layout follows this pattern:

```
cmd/content-api/        # HTTP server entrypoint + wiring only
internal/
  domain/               # pure domain types + sentinel errors (no I/O)
  port/                 # interfaces (ContentStore port)
  adapter/
    postgres/           # pgx adapter (metadata + inline payload bytes)
    http/               # net/http REST adapter
  config/               # environment-first configuration loading
  security/             # tenant context + audit log writer
pkg/
  digest/               # SHA-256 helpers (sha256:<hex>)
  reference/            # v1 ContentReference encode/parse
test/
  integration/          # testcontainers-based tests (build tag: integration)
tests/
  conformance/          # Design 1.26 conformance checks
```

**Rules:**

- `internal/domain/` must not import any package that performs I/O. If you reach for `pgx`, `net/http`, or S3 SDKs inside domain, define a port instead.
- Adapters depend on ports (interfaces), never the reverse. `cmd/` wires concrete adapters.
- Tenant checks belong on every read, write, and metadata path. Cross-tenant failures must be indistinguishable (`ErrNotFound`, same message, no timing leak).

## Build System

Standard Go toolchain plus `make` targets (`Makefile`):

```bash
make build             # version-stamped binary (VERSION file is the source of truth)
make test              # unit tests with -race
make test-integration  # testcontainers suite (needs Docker)
make test-conformance  # Design 1.26 Core checks
make vet               # go vet
make bench             # digest + reference benchmarks
```

Key commands:

```bash
go build ./...
go test -race ./...
go test -race -tags=integration ./test/integration/... -v
```

> **Do not run plain `go mod tidy`.** Under go 1.22, tidy prunes the dependencies of `integration`-tagged test files (e.g. testcontainers) from `go.mod`, silently breaking `make test-integration` for the next person. Add adapter dependencies with explicit `go get module@version` instead.

## Guidelines

When generating Go code:

- Make the minimum change possible to complete the task.
- Wrap errors with context (`fmt.Errorf("...: %w", err)`); use sentinel errors in `internal/domain` (`ErrNotFound`, `ErrTenantMismatch`, `ErrIntegrityMismatch`) and compare with `errors.Is`.
- Prefer table-driven tests for domain logic; use fake clocks, never `time.Sleep` in tests.
- Always run with `-race` (unit and integration). Race detector must be clean.
- Configuration is environment-first (`CONTENT_*`), validated in `Load()`; fail fast with the cause on stderr before listeners bind.
- PostgreSQL via `pgx/v5` (`pgxpool`). Dedup writes use `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` / `DO UPDATE`; multi-table writes share one transaction.
- A foreign key must reference a `PRIMARY KEY` or `UNIQUE` **constraint** — a bare `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX` does not satisfy Postgres FKs. Origin: `reference_registry` FK against `artifacts(tenant_id, digest)` failed until `uq_artifacts_tenant_digest` became a constraint (2026-10-13).
- Migrations are plain `*.up.sql` / `*.down.sql` applied lexically; the service applies `.up.sql` only on `CONTENT_MIGRATE_ON_STARTUP=true`. Rollbacks are manual, reverse order.
- Keep comments concise. Never use comments for chain-of-thought; code must be self-explanatory with short doc comments on exported identifiers only.
